Introducing Early Abandoning (EA) into brute-force or index-based time series similarity searches has historically been an elusive goal, primarily due to the contrasting characteristics of time series data. Unlike standard datasets, time series are typically continuous streams with varying lengths that arrive continuously in real-time contexts. However, the widespread adoption EA has seen recently across numerous domains. This study presents principles and techniques specifically tailored to make EA viable for time series similarity search.

Consequently, the authors offer a comprehensive, comparative evaluation of EA's efficacy by:
*   Investigating how various factors influence its performance;
*   Assessing its suitability across different datasets and similarity measures;
*   Examining its efficacy in both continuous streams and stored collections.

Comprehensive experiments were conducted on four time series streams alongside 557 time series collections containing over 567k series with lengths exceeding 567k. These experiments utilized a combination of Euclidean, Frechet, Dynamic Time Warping, and multiple masking strategies.

